   "answer": "**Result: mCherry-positive cells, HDR versus HITI-2c**\n\nHDR is homology-directed repair. HITI-2c is homology-independent targeted integration, design 2c.\n\n- HDR: 4.42 ± 0.15 % mCherry-positive (mean ± SD, n = 3).\n- HITI-2c: 20.90 ± 2.26 % mCherry-positive (mean ± SD, n = 3).\n- Difference, HITI-2c minus HDR: 16.48 percentage points.\n- Welch t test: p = 0.0060. There was one comparison, so no correction was needed.\n\nThe tool results do not show the software version. I removed the version number from this answer.\n\n**Cutoff**\n- The mCherry-positive cutoff is 3791.2 on the PE-CF594-A linear scale.\n- The source is the three untransfected control files, pooled.\n- The tool did not report the control percentile. It comes from the decision record, so I cannot confirm its value here.\n\n**Per sample** (one sample file equals one replicate)\n\n| Sample | Parent events | Positive events | % of parent | Median of positive events (MFI) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HDR rep1 | 15688 | 680 | 4.33 | 23660.7 |\n| HDR rep2 | 17504 | 803 | 4.59 | 23249.2 |\n| HDR rep3 | 17158 | 744 | 4.34 | 23016.6 |\n| HITI-2c rep1 | 16334 | 3286 | 20.12 | 26088.9 |\n| HITI-2c rep2 | 15815 | 3708 | 23.45 | 28286.8 |\n| HITI-2c rep3 | 14657 | 2806 | 19.14 | 29720.6 |\n\nThe untransfected controls give 0.18 %, 0.02 % and 0.10 % positive events with the same cutoff (rep1 to rep3).\n\n**Steps**\n1. Checked the nine FCS files and the sample sheet. The sheet groups files only as bv and control, so I made a design file with HDR and HITI-2c.\n2. Set the cutoff from the three untransfected controls, pooled. Linear data, no compensation, no transform.\n3. Counted the mCherry-positive events in the six bv files with population_stats. The parent gate was all events.\n4. Compared the designs with compare_groups, Welch t test, on percent of parent.\n\n**Uncertain**\n- The cutoff depends on the control percentile, which the tool did not show. A different percentile changes all percents.\n- Each group has n = 3. If the three tubes came from one transfection batch, the p value overstates how independent the replicates are.\n- The median of positive events is listed for each sample. I did not test the difference between designs for this value.\n\n**Waiting for you**\n- Confirm the control percentile in the decision record.\n- Confirm whether the three replicates are separate transfections on separate days.\n\nStep results that the answer does not mention, from the record:\n- n2 run_script: The script ran in /Volumes/T7/guided-analysis/tools/bench-runs/2026-10-09/claude_claude-haiku-5-5/aulicino2022-baculovirus-hdr/run1/home/sessions/20261009-040540-ac5a/work and wrote 1 new file(s) to /Volumes/T7/guided-analysis/tools/bench-runs/2026-10-09/claude_claude-haiku-5-5/aulicino2022-baculovirus-hdr/run1/home/sessions/20261009-040540-ac5a/work.\n\nSettings used, from the decision record: Decades of a log-amplified scale: 0 · Parent gate on scatter: none · Compensation of the samples: none · Transform of the fluorescence channels: none · Cofactor of the asinh transform: 150 · Cutoff between negative and positive: none · Percentile of the control for the cutoff: 99.9 · Statistical test for the groups: welch · Correction for many comparisons: none.",
